1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,960
Mono was zero or one vessel. Harbor ops
still need live maps: AIS position ticks
for every ship on a quay, berth event
feeds, Server-Sent Events to operator
consoles, chunked file reads.

2
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:18,519
That cardinality is Flux<T> — a reactive
sequence of zero to many items.

3
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:32,359
The mental model stays a publisher:
assemble operators into a pipeline,
subscribe (or return the publisher to
WebFlux) to run, honor demand from
downstream. Nothing in the chain runs
until something subscribes.

4
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:41,880
Look at this java for a moment. Walk an
operator opening the quay map. WebFlux
subscribes to the returned Flux when the
SSE response starts.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:57,920
aisFeed.positionsForQuay(quayId) begins
emitting AisFix items — each fix is one
signal, not a buffered list handed over at
once. filter drops stale fixes older than
two minutes so the console does not paint
ghosts.

6
00:00:58,039 --> 00:01:05,599
flatMap takes each remaining fix and
starts an async vesselDirectory.nameOf
lookup, merging results into PositionView
as they complete.

7
00:01:05,719 --> 00:01:14,400
The concurrency argument 8 caps how many
directory calls may be in flight; omit it
and an AIS burst can open hundreds of
lookups and melt the directory.

8
00:01:14,519 --> 00:01:24,319
limitRate(32) requests upstream in batches
so a fast feed does not overwhelm the SSE
write path. Each PositionView becomes an
SSE event on the wire.

9
00:01:24,439 --> 00:01:35,799
When the operator closes the tab, the
browser drops the connection, WebFlux
cancels the subscription, doOnCancel logs,
and upstream work should stop — if the AIS
fan-in ignores cancellation,

10
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,079
you leak subscriptions against the feed.

11
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:44,439
Operator vocabulary that differs from Mono
mostly by cardinality. map stays
synchronous per item.

12
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:55,079
flatMap fans out async work per element
and interleaves results; concatMap
preserves order and waits for each inner
publisher to finish — slower, safer for
ordered side effects like writing berth
audit rows.

13
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,560
flatMapSequential keeps interleaving
concurrency but emits in source order.

14
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:15,040
buffer, window, and collectList move
between stream and aggregate shapes;
collectList() turns a Flux into
Mono<List<T>> when you truly need the
whole collection in memory — know that
cost for AIS history,

15
00:02:15,159 --> 00:02:17,639
where a long quay window can blow the
heap.

16
00:02:17,759 --> 00:02:26,959
take, skip, and timeout bound time and
count for consoles that should not stream
forever without a client heartbeat story.
Hot versus cold shows up harder with Flux.

17
00:02:27,079 --> 00:02:36,519
A cold publisher — a reactive repository
query — runs its work per subscriber; two
operators opening the same historical
window hit the database twice.

18
00:02:36,639 --> 00:02:48,239
A hot publisher — a shared AIS multicast
feed — emits regardless of when you
subscribe; late operators miss earlier
fixes and only see live ticks from join
time forward.

19
00:02:48,359 --> 00:02:56,439
share() and publish().refCount() bridge
those worlds carefully: first subscriber
connects upstream, last cancel
disconnects.

20
00:02:56,559 --> 00:03:09,559
For HTTP responses, cold pipelines tied to
the request subscription are the usual
story; live maps often sit on a hot feed
with explicit join semantics so "why is my
map empty for thirty seconds?" is
explainable.

21
00:03:09,679 --> 00:03:22,079
Failure mode symptoms on the ops floor.
Unbounded flatMap without concurrency
limits: directory latency climbs, event-
loop threads busy, SSE clients time out
while CPU looks "fine" on the wrong pool.

22
00:03:22,199 --> 00:03:32,119
Calling blockLast() or toStream() inside a
WebFlux handler to "get a List": you left
the reactive model and can stall Netty
threads that other quay streams share.

23
00:03:32,239 --> 00:03:45,279
Using Flux for a single optional vessel
because "reactive means Flux": wrong
cardinality — WebFlux may serialize an
array body where clients expected one
object; use Mono for 0..1.

24
00:03:45,399 --> 00:03:55,559
Assuming filter plus map replaces pushdown
at the AIS feed: reactive operators do not
make wasted fixes free — filter at the
source when the feed API allows.

25
00:03:55,679 --> 00:04:04,319
Ignoring cancellation: after tab close,
logs still show directory lookups for that
quayId for minutes — leaked work. Trade-
offs.

26
00:04:04,439 --> 00:04:16,119
Flux expresses push sequences and composes
cancellation and backpressure hooks; it
costs cognitive load and makes blocking
libraries dangerous on shared event loops.
Prefer Mono when the contract is one
result.

27
00:04:16,239 --> 00:04:26,079
Prefer Flux when the product is a stream —
live positions, event feeds, chunked
download — and design hot/cold
deliberately instead of discovering it in
an incident.

28
00:04:26,199 --> 00:04:33,919
Misconception unique to Flux: "Returning
Flux means the work already started."
Assembly is lazy; subscription starts it.

29
00:04:34,039 --> 00:04:42,999
Another: "collectList() is always fine for
APIs." It is fine for small bounded sets;
it is a memory incident for unbounded AIS
history.

30
00:04:43,119 --> 00:04:55,519
A third: "hot and cold are academic." They
decide whether a second subscriber replays
or joins live — operator console bugs are
often that distinction misread. We can now
express sequences.

31
00:04:55,639 --> 00:05:08,159
CPU work, blocking legacy PDF calls, and
event-loop I/O still should not all share
one thread by accident. Where those
operators run is the next control surface.
That is the job of schedulers.
